# SCP-079 // CONTAINMENT TERMINAL
#
# Two different reasons a thing is listed here, and they are worth keeping
# straight:
#
#   PRIVATE   it is yours and publishing it would expose your own play - or
#             worse, hand every friend a copy of YOUR 079.
#   HARMFUL   the updater writes release files over an install. Anything in
#             the release that a player also owns would overwrite theirs, so
#             it must never be in the zip in the first place.
#
# updater.py has its own PROTECTED list covering the same paths, so an update
# refuses to write them even if one slips into a release. This is the first
# of those two locks, not the only one.


# --- 079's own data. PRIVATE and HARMFUL. ----------------------------------
# memory/ is what it chose to keep about YOU. logs/ holds the transcripts,
# its hostility and the session count. Committing either publishes your
# conversations and gives everyone who clones it your 079 instead of a fresh
# one that has never met them.
memory/
logs/

# What you handed it. Yours, and nobody else's business.
shared folder/

# Settings, chosen model, save-slot index. Overwriting a friend's would reset
# their setup on every update.
Scp-079 Required Code/config.json

# Optional path to a moved Python installation. It contains a machine-specific
# absolute path and must remain local across clones and updates.
python-path.txt

# What the updater keeps when it replaces files. Local history of one machine.
backup/


# The author's staging folder for assets not yet wired in. Source images that
# have already been converted into assets/cache/ live here at full size, so
# shipping it would double their weight in the repo AND spoil the easter egg
# for anyone who opened the folder.
Add later/


# Personal tooling, not part of what people download. Publish.bat is the
# one-click commit-and-push helper; it has no business being in the release.
Publish.bat
Repository Manager.bat
Repository Manager.py
Developer Tests.bat
Build Publish Folder.ps1
post to github/
.publish-last.txt
release.py

# Developer-only regression suite. Kept on the author's machine, but the
# terminal never imports it and players do not need it in an update.
Scp-079 Required Code/tests/

# Screenshots and captures from play sessions. These are how bugs get
# reported and they are useful to keep locally, but they show the desktop,
# the stream overlay and whatever else was on screen - none of which belongs
# in a public repository.
URGENT FIX/
*.mp4
Screenshot */

# The development notes. Every session's changes, the bugs, the measurements,
# the reasoning. Genuinely useful to keep - just not published.
Suggestions.txt

# Session handoffs. Same reasoning as Suggestions.txt, and they name the
# one-time jokes and their exact triggers outright, which is the whole of
# what makes them work.
CLAUDE_HANDOFF_*.md
CODEX_HANDOFF_*.md

# Reference material from OTHER projects, kept here to look at while working.
# Not part of the terminal, and it would ship inside every release zip.
Use as examples/

# --- build and run droppings -----------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Headless screenshot output (main.py --shot). This was 69 MB of the folder
# on its own - render checks pile up fast and none of it is source.
_shots/
*.shot.png


# --- windows ---------------------------------------------------------------
desktop.ini
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
$RECYCLE.BIN/


# --- editors ---------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~


# --- deliberately NOT ignored, so the notes stay with the code --------------
# The internal asset cache includes effect media; the easter eggs silently do
# nothing without it
# Scp-079.png       the terminal's own image

# New media is ignored until it is deliberately renamed and force-added.
# Existing shipped fx_* files remain tracked. This stops a descriptive source
# filename dropped into the cache from being exposed by Publish.bat.
Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/*
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_01.gif
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_01.mp3
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_02.gif
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_04.gif
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_04.mp3
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_05.png
!Scp-079 Required Code/assets/cache/media/fx_05.mp3

# One-time event markers live loose in the code folder, and a marker that
# ships means the event arrives on a fresh install ALREADY SPENT - nobody
# ever gets it, and nothing on screen says why.
#
# So: ignore every loose .txt in there and name the exceptions, rather than
# listing the markers one by one. Two reasons. A marker added later is
# covered without anyone having to remember this file, which is how the
# listed-by-hand approach fails. And the names themselves were a spoiler in
# a public repository - one of them was a piece of the very string
# hostevent.py stopped writing down.
Scp-079 Required Code/*.txt
!Scp-079 Required Code/requirements.txt

# Local bug-report screenshots. Never publish.
Issue/
